---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import EntityReference from '../../components/EntityReference.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="HTML Entities List — Character Codes Reference" description="Searchable list of HTML character entities and their codes — symbols, punctuation, accented letters, and special characters.">
  <EntityReference client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Common HTML Entities List</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Character</th><th>Entity Name</th><th>Numeric Code</th><th>Description</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>&lt;</td><td><code>&amp;lt;</code></td><td><code>&amp;#60;</code></td><td>Less than</td></tr>
      <tr><td>&gt;</td><td><code>&amp;gt;</code></td><td><code>&amp;#62;</code></td><td>Greater than</td></tr>
      <tr><td>&amp;</td><td><code>&amp;amp;</code></td><td><code>&amp;#38;</code></td><td>Ampersand</td></tr>
      <tr><td>&quot;</td><td><code>&amp;quot;</code></td><td><code>&amp;#34;</code></td><td>Double quote</td></tr>
      <tr><td>&copy;</td><td><code>&amp;copy;</code></td><td><code>&amp;#169;</code></td><td>Copyright</td></tr>
      <tr><td>&reg;</td><td><code>&amp;reg;</code></td><td><code>&amp;#174;</code></td><td>Registered trademark</td></tr>
      <tr><td>&nbsp;</td><td><code>&amp;nbsp;</code></td><td><code>&amp;#160;</code></td><td>Non-breaking space</td></tr>
      <tr><td>&euro;</td><td><code>&amp;euro;</code></td><td><code>&amp;#8364;</code></td><td>Euro sign</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The searchable tool above covers the full list, including accented letters, arrows, and mathematical symbols — this table shows the entities people look up most often.</p>

  <h2>Why entities still matter in modern HTML</h2>
  <p>UTF-8 has made most special characters directly typeable in HTML source, but entities remain necessary for reserved characters like <code>&lt;</code> and <code>&amp;</code>, and useful as a fallback when your editor or encoding setup doesn't handle a special character cleanly.</p>

  <h2>How to use this reference</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Search for the character or symbol you need.</li>
    <li>Click to copy its HTML entity code.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common HTML Entity Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Forgetting to escape <code>&lt;</code> and <code>&amp;</code></strong> when displaying literal text that happens to contain these characters, which browsers otherwise interpret as markup.</li>
    <li><strong>Double-escaping</strong> — encoding an already-encoded entity, producing visible entity codes instead of the intended symbol.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When You'd Reach for an HTML Entity</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Displaying a literal <code>&lt;</code> or <code>&gt;</code> character in rendered HTML without it being parsed as a tag</li>
    <li>Adding a copyright, trademark, or currency symbol to a page</li>
    <li>Inserting a non-breaking space to prevent unwanted line breaks</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>HTML Entity FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>What is the HTML entity for a dot or period?</strong> A plain period doesn't need escaping — it's not a reserved HTML character. If you need a middle dot symbol specifically, that's <code>&amp;middot;</code> (&middot;).</p>
  <p><strong>Do I need entities for all special characters now?</strong> Not for most — UTF-8 handles the vast majority directly; entities remain essential mainly for HTML's own reserved characters.</p>
  <p><strong>What's the difference between named and numeric entities?</strong> Named entities like <code>&amp;copy;</code> are easier to read; numeric entities like <code>&amp;#169;</code> work everywhere named entities are supported and also cover characters without a named equivalent.</p>
</ToolLayout>
